1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,300
If you're wondering how far or how long,

2
00:00:03,301 --> 00:00:06,300
how heavy, how fast, or how cold,

3
00:00:06,301 --> 00:00:08,400
when you need to know what you're working with,

4
00:00:08,401 --> 00:00:09,800
you've got to measure it.

5
00:00:09,801 --> 00:00:12,000
From radar guns to atomic clocks,

6
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
speedometers to whale scales,

7
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
the high-precision tools that bear the heaviest loads,

8
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,200
feel the heat, and respond to the slightest touch,

9
00:00:20,201 --> 00:00:23,000
allow us to build our civilization.

10
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, measure it on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,540
Your alarm clock erupts.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,180
It's chilly, so you crank up the thermostat before making breakfast.

13
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:49,420
You flip on the news to see what the weather will be like outside.

14
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,620
It's not even 8 a.m., and you've already encountered a series of measurements.

15
00:00:54,621 --> 00:01:00,100
and the rest of your day will be guided by an inescapable string of numbers that define your

16
00:01:00,101 --> 00:01:06,140
world. In your car, at the office, on the road, and even on the bathroom floor.

17
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:07,440
Nearly everywhere

18
00:01:07,441 --> 00:01:13,100
you go and everything you do depends on measurements. They're what lifted us from

19
00:01:13,101 --> 00:01:15,515
the Stone Age into civilization.

20
00:01:15,516 --> 00:01:20,420
To ensure that we get the right amount of fruit, gas, or postage,

21
00:01:20,421 --> 00:01:25,060
the National Institute of Standards and Technology, or NIST,

22
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:28,120
defines all of these measurements and more.

23
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:34,760
We are responsible for what's known as realization of the primary units of measurement.

24
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:39,720
We are charged with the research and the dissemination of the basic units

25
00:01:39,721 --> 00:01:41,820
by which we all measure our lives.

26
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:47,680
NIST was born out of tragedies like the Great Baltimore Fire of 1904,

27
00:01:47,681 --> 00:01:54,120
which wouldn't have been such a disaster if all the United States were operating under a standardized set of measurements.

28
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:59,540
During that fire, 1,500 buildings burnt to the ground.

29
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,968
The Baltimore Fire Department called for help, and crews came from Washington, D.C.

30
00:02:04,969 --> 00:02:05,980
and New York City.

31
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:12,040
But when they arrived in Baltimore City, they found that their hose couplings did not match the couplings of Baltimore fire hydrants.

32
00:02:12,041 --> 00:02:21,080
And so they were not able to help, and the importance of standards for things like hose couplings and fire hydrant fixtures became very, very apparent.

33
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:36,144
For the seven base units of measurement, length, mass, time, electrical current, temperature, moles, or the number of elemental bodies in a substance, and luminous

34
00:02:36,145 --> 00:02:39,320
intensity, the standards are all here.

35
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:43,260
NIST maintains the meter, our critical length standard.

36
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:50,340
In 1791, the French Academy of Sciences divided the longitude from the North Pole to the equator,

37
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,340
through Paris, into 10 million parts.

38
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:55,880
Each fraction represented one meter.

39
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:02,020
In 1889, the International Bureau of Weights and Measures created a meter bar

40
00:03:02,021 --> 00:03:05,540
made of a platinum iridium alloy to act as the standard.

41
00:03:05,541 --> 00:03:14,140
This is the United States actual meter bar, the one that every length measurement was traced to for over 90 years.

42
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,440
The meter bar proved impractical.

43
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:24,980
The metal alloy is worth over $100,000, but it's still susceptible to wear, and therefore change.

44
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:30,340
So in 1983, the world's measurement community agreed to redefine the meter

45
00:03:30,341 --> 00:03:33,200
as the distance light travels in a vacuum

46
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:40,200
in one 299,792,458th of a second.

47
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:43,900
But hold on.

48
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:46,960
Why does America care so much about a unit of measurement

49
00:03:46,961 --> 00:03:48,240
it doesn't readily use?

50
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:51,960
It's been estimated, in fact, that U.S. manufacturers,

51
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:55,120
about 75% of them, are totally metric.

52
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:59,440
For Americans, the continued use of the inch-pound system

53
00:03:59,441 --> 00:04:01,120
is merely a historical holdover.

54
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,880
As colonists from Great Britain,

55
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:06,140
we didn't have our own measurement system,

56
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:08,440
so we brought what we were familiar with

57
00:04:08,441 --> 00:04:10,480
when we came to the United States.

58
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:15,340
The U.S. customary system is mathematically impractical.

59
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:18,500
Twelve inches are in a foot,

60
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:20,320
three feet are in a yard,

61
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,240
and 1,760 yards are in a mile.

62
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:29,400
Conversely, the base unit of length in the metric system

63
00:04:29,401 --> 00:04:33,660
is a meter and its other units are expressed in multiples of 10.

64
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:37,860
A kilometer is a thousand meters.

65
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:43,480
Regardless of whether you're working with metric or the U.S. customary system,

66
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:47,340
your measurement is only as accurate as the instrument being used.

67
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:54,300
The standard length measurement regulated by NIST

68
00:04:54,301 --> 00:04:57,080
is the basis for how companies like Stanley Tools

69
00:04:57,081 --> 00:05:01,720
create professional and consumer distance measuring devices.

70
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:04,880
At its manufacturing facility in New Britain, Connecticut,

71
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:08,320
workers roll out more than 10 million tape measures a year.

72
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:12,220
Stanley was founded in 1843 really

73
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:15,840
around hardware products, which are hinges and the like,

74
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:17,840
by Frederick T. Stanley and his brother.

75
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:20,340
The old adage, measure twice and cut once,

76
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:21,940
has been with us forever.

77
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:23,840
And if you're inaccurate in what you do,

78
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:25,840
then every other part of that project

79
00:05:25,841 --> 00:05:29,680
project or that development will be incorrect.

80
00:05:29,681 --> 00:05:31,580
Each step in the manufacturing process

81
00:05:31,581 --> 00:05:33,540
is essential to the final product's ability

82
00:05:33,541 --> 00:05:36,680
to take an accurate length measurement.

83
00:05:36,681 --> 00:05:38,660
We're at the slitter, which is the beginning

84
00:05:38,661 --> 00:05:40,220
of our tape roll operation.

85
00:05:40,221 --> 00:05:42,860
This is where we take wide band material

86
00:05:42,861 --> 00:05:45,520
and we slid it into whatever size our customer requires.

87
00:05:45,521 --> 00:05:48,140
Each tape roll has a blade, and it also

88
00:05:48,141 --> 00:05:49,880
has a spring that's about two thirds

89
00:05:49,881 --> 00:05:50,880
of the length of a blade.

90
00:05:50,881 --> 00:05:53,600
So what we have here is a tool that's actually

91
00:05:53,601 --> 00:05:58,880
slitting seven pieces of one inch wide tape rule material.

92
00:05:58,881 --> 00:06:03,360
These massive steel wheels will become individual tape measures.

93
00:06:03,361 --> 00:06:08,980
To make the tool easier for one person to use, the tape measure has to have good standout.

94
00:06:08,981 --> 00:06:12,600
Standout is how far can this blade measure something with one person, essentially.

95
00:06:12,601 --> 00:06:15,840
How long will it stand out on its own?

96
00:06:15,841 --> 00:06:21,240
This product will stand out 11 feet, which is an industry best.

97
00:06:21,241 --> 00:06:23,140
To achieve a desirable level of standout,

98
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:25,700
the steel blades for the spring and the tape rule

99
00:06:25,701 --> 00:06:28,260
must be heat-treated and tempered after they're slit.

100
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:33,920
What we're doing here is we're hardening the material in this furnace.

101
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:36,080
It's coming up to like 1,800 degrees.

102
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:37,860
Then we're quenching it with water.

103
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:40,580
This process also gives the tape rule its curve.

104
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:43,980
After the blades have their bath,

105
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,540
machines apply the signature yellow paint

106
00:06:46,541 --> 00:06:48,380
to the entire length of the tape rule.

107
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:50,760
Then workers haul them off to printing.

108
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,880
These are two bands that we print our tape rolls with.

109
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:57,260
One band is black that we put graduations on.

110
00:06:57,340 --> 00:06:58,540
That's the important part of printing.

111
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:01,560
The second band is the red band that are the graphics.

112
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:04,940
The so-called laser interferometer,

113
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:09,120
utilized by Stanley Tools to ensure the accuracy of each of its tape measures,

114
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:11,900
is traceable to the NIST standardized meter.

115
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:17,140
If the graduations on the rule are off,

116
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:20,800
Stanley can compress or stretch the bands to adjust the print.

117
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:26,840
After we've printed a coil, we take one blade, bring it into this gauge, and this gauge at

118
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:30,720
pre-described increments measures from four inches to the end of the blade.

119
00:07:30,721 --> 00:07:35,420
If this sample is accurate, then all the tape rolls from the previous sample are also accurate.

120
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:39,600
This is our in-process check to ensure the accuracy of everything that we manufacture.

121
00:07:39,601 --> 00:07:43,200
The device actually measures to the center of every single graduation.

122
00:07:43,201 --> 00:07:48,440
As an example, any particular rule setup has variances along the length, and this machine

123
00:07:48,441 --> 00:07:53,840
you will find five discrete points and check for tolerance at that point.

124
00:07:53,841 --> 00:08:00,200
Thousands of years before NIST and the rest of the world agreed on length standards for the graduations on this tape,

125
00:08:00,201 --> 00:08:05,080
ancient civilizations were creating their own methods for measuring distance.

126
00:08:05,081 --> 00:08:13,680
In the Fertile Crescent, as early as 5000 BC, the Sumerians created the first units of length based on human anatomy.

127
00:08:13,681 --> 00:08:15,980
The distance from the elbow to the fingertips

128
00:08:15,981 --> 00:08:20,980
was called a cue and is still known today as the cubit.

129
00:08:20,981 --> 00:08:24,520
The ancient Egyptians borrowed the cubit from the Sumerians.

130
00:08:24,521 --> 00:08:28,200
Thousands and thousands of workers who built the pyramids

131
00:08:28,201 --> 00:08:31,540
were able to take transfers from this master cubit

132
00:08:31,541 --> 00:08:34,300
and do the design and the construction of the pyramid.

133
00:08:34,301 --> 00:08:37,280
And in fact, the pyramid was built within an accuracy

134
00:08:37,281 --> 00:08:39,540
of 0.05%.

135
00:08:39,541 --> 00:08:43,180
Using these wooden sticks that were calibrated

136
00:08:43,181 --> 00:08:46,020
of the master royal granite cubit.

137
00:08:46,021 --> 00:08:49,420
The calibration, in fact, was required to take place once

138
00:08:49,421 --> 00:08:52,180
every moon under penalty of death.

139
00:08:52,181 --> 00:08:54,020
We have somewhat less stringent requirements

140
00:08:54,021 --> 00:08:57,020
for recalibration intervals now.

141
00:08:57,021 --> 00:08:59,520
Ancient scribes and engineers all over the world

142
00:08:59,521 --> 00:09:02,440
use the human figure measurements.

143
00:09:02,441 --> 00:09:05,440
We get our inch from an old English measurement,

144
00:09:05,441 --> 00:09:09,840
the width of a man's thumb at the base of the nail.

145
00:09:09,841 --> 00:09:13,980
We actually get our foot from the length of a man's foot.

146
00:09:13,981 --> 00:09:16,480
Our yard could have been the length of a man's nose

147
00:09:16,481 --> 00:09:18,720
to his fingertips.

148
00:09:18,721 --> 00:09:23,520
And our mile, or mele passum, was devised by the Romans

149
00:09:23,521 --> 00:09:25,740
and literally translated to 1,000 paces.

150
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,620
In reality, the variations among these measurements

151
00:09:30,621 --> 00:09:34,180
were vast, but they still led to cultural advancement

152
00:09:34,181 --> 00:09:36,340
and expanded trade.

153
00:09:36,341 --> 00:09:38,980
Empires had to agree on how to measure their goods

154
00:09:38,981 --> 00:09:42,420
in order to price them in a marketplace with ever-growing borders.

155
00:09:42,421 --> 00:09:46,920
But today, the comparability community, if you will, is now worldwide.

156
00:09:46,921 --> 00:09:49,920
And so these local standards don't do us any good anymore.

157
00:09:49,921 --> 00:09:53,880
We need globally accepted standards.

158
00:09:53,881 --> 00:09:57,740
And that takes us back to Stanley and its strict adherence to NIST standards.

159
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:04,540
If the tape rule sample is good, workers load the rolls into machines

160
00:10:04,541 --> 00:10:08,120
that glue on a protective mylar film.

161
00:10:08,121 --> 00:10:11,460
Machines then cut the rolls into 250 individual measuring tapes.

162
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:20,100
Each tape is inspected before assembly to weed out any physical abnormalities that would affect its ability to take an accurate measurement.

163
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:29,380
If they find an anomaly, not only do they select that blade out for a strap, but they also determine the root cause so we can correct that problem.

164
00:10:29,381 --> 00:10:32,520
It involves some 87 different attributes that they look for.

165
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:40,820
Workers move approved blades onto the assembly line,

166
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:42,760
where they're hooked to their springs,

167
00:10:44,180 --> 00:10:46,340
encased, screwed tight,

168
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:48,340
and packaged for shipment.

169
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:52,260
Stanley may turn out a high-precision tape rule,

170
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:55,680
but it's only good for measuring relatively short distances.

171
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:00,560
How do you measure the distance around something really big

172
00:11:00,561 --> 00:11:02,900
or to something really far away?

173
00:11:02,901 --> 00:11:10,240
Surveyors have used triangulation since the 6th century BC to measure the heights of mountains

174
00:11:10,241 --> 00:11:12,960
and sea islands in order to make better maps.

175
00:11:12,961 --> 00:11:15,680
First, they find the distance to the mountain

176
00:11:15,681 --> 00:11:20,572
by measuring angles to it from locations at either end of a fixed baseline.

177
00:11:20,573 --> 00:11:21,420
Then they use

178
00:11:21,421 --> 00:11:24,999
a known altitude to find the angle to the top of the mountain.

179
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,020
With that angle, they can calculate

180
00:11:27,021 --> 00:11:28,775
the mountain's height.

181
00:11:28,776 --> 00:11:33,960
Over 2,000 years ago, Eratosthenes cleverly used triangulation to

182
00:11:33,961 --> 00:11:39,380
measure the circumference of the Earth. His calculation was off by less than 1%.

183
00:11:39,381 --> 00:11:47,920
And even today, astronomers use triangulation to measure a star's distance from the Earth.

184
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:53,860
Since stars are so far away, their distance is measured in light years.

185
00:11:53,861 --> 00:11:59,800
The distance light speeding at 186,000 miles a second travels in one year.

186
00:12:01,340 --> 00:12:05,680
So we can measure the distance to a star because we can measure time.

187
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:11,140
And we get that essential time measurement from the most accurate clock on Earth.

188
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:17,360
But why does the world need a clock 100 million times more accurate than the one on your wrist?

189
00:12:17,361 --> 00:12:20,651
In the 1960s, NIST moved from Washington, D.C.

190
00:12:20,652 --> 00:12:32,100
to Gaithersburg, Maryland, in part because the vibrations from city traffic were strong enough to affect the organization's ability to take proper measurements.

191
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:35,860
Measure It will return on Modern Marvels.

192
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:40,900
We now return to Measure It on Modern Marvels.

193
00:12:43,180 --> 00:12:45,320
Our lives are ruled by measurements.

194
00:12:45,321 --> 00:12:51,320
If you don't think so, try to get through your day without looking at a clock.

195
00:12:51,321 --> 00:12:54,320
We measure time as if our lives depend on them.

196
00:12:54,321 --> 00:12:58,320
And they do.

197
00:12:58,321 --> 00:13:02,320
Leave it to NIST to build the world's most accurate timekeeping instrument,

198
00:13:02,321 --> 00:13:05,320
the NIST F1 atomic clock.

199
00:13:05,321 --> 00:13:08,320
So how accurate is the most accurate clock on Earth?

200
00:13:08,321 --> 00:13:13,320
We originally said, well, we want to get an accuracy of about 10 to the minus 15 out of this clock,

201
00:13:13,321 --> 00:13:16,080
and it's now at 3 times 10 to the minus 16.

202
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:23,620
And that means that's the equivalent of the clock losing

203
00:13:23,621 --> 00:13:26,560
or gaining one second in about 100 million years.

204
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:30,500
Stephen Jeffords, with NIST's Time and Frequency Division,

205
00:13:31,120 --> 00:13:34,200
has led the F1 project from its inception in 1996.

206
00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:38,360
We call this an atomic clock, and it's really not a clock

207
00:13:38,361 --> 00:13:41,000
in the way that you tend to think of a clock.

208
00:13:41,001 --> 00:13:43,480
It doesn't have hands, and it doesn't tell time.

209
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:46,740
All it does is tell me how long a second is.

210
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:52,000
It doesn't put a label on the second to say that it's 5 o'clock in the afternoon.

211
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:57,260
It will just tell me the difference between 5 o'clock and 5001.

212
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:01,880
The standard second, like the meter, is based on a natural phenomenon,

213
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:08,880
the oscillation between the nucleus of a cesium-133 atom and its surrounding electrons.

214
00:14:08,881 --> 00:14:14,920
Before the first atomic clock, NIST faced the second on astronomical observations,

215
00:14:14,921 --> 00:14:20,060
and it was recorded as one 86,400th of a solar day.

216
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:28,100
But by the early 1900s, researchers had already concluded that the measurement was problematic.

217
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:33,920
So it turns out that the rotation of the Earth on its axis is not a very good clock.

218
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:37,820
The Earth wobbles, it doesn't rotate at the same speed all the time.

219
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:40,680
Sometimes it slows down, sometimes it speeds up.

220
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:44,400
And it turns out that the rate between the atomic clocks

221
00:14:44,401 --> 00:14:46,500
and using the Earth as a clock

222
00:14:46,501 --> 00:14:49,840
has differed by about 30 seconds over this 40 years.

223
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:51,780
And 30 seconds is not that big a deal.

224
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:54,260
But if you let this continue indefinitely,

225
00:14:54,261 --> 00:14:57,520
you end up with sunrise being in the afternoon

226
00:14:57,521 --> 00:14:58,300
and things like that.

227
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:01,720
The solution came in the late 1940s,

228
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:04,760
while Nobel Prize-winning physicist Norman Ramsey

229
00:15:04,761 --> 00:15:07,320
was studying the frequency oscillations of atoms.

230
00:15:07,820 --> 00:15:11,820
And along the line, he said, hey, wait a minute.

231
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,240
If I can measure these frequencies this accurately,

232
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:17,100
I can turn this around and turn it into a clock.

233
00:15:17,260 --> 00:15:18,780
I must know how fast it's ticking,

234
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:20,880
because the ticking rate is the frequency.

235
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:25,260
In 1967, the world agreed to a new standard,

236
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:26,800
the atomic second.

237
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:35,800
The atomic second equals 9,192,631,770 oscillations

238
00:15:35,801 --> 00:15:40,900
oscillations in a cesium-133 atom.

239
00:15:40,901 --> 00:15:42,840
But building a device to accurately measure those

240
00:15:42,841 --> 00:15:45,940
oscillations wasn't easy.

241
00:15:45,941 --> 00:15:49,140
The clock must be able to first select only cesium atoms in

242
00:15:49,141 --> 00:15:52,820
their lowest energy state, then excite them into a higher

243
00:15:52,821 --> 00:15:55,780
energy state.

244
00:15:55,781 --> 00:15:59,900
The cesium starts inside here, gets shot through this tube

245
00:15:59,901 --> 00:16:03,820
inside the clock, at which point there's a vapor, a gas of

246
00:16:03,821 --> 00:16:05,370
cesium atoms in there.

247
00:16:05,371 --> 00:16:10,300
Six lasers directed into the F1's main chamber control the movement of

248
00:16:10,301 --> 00:16:16,457
the cesium atoms during the experiment. The next chamber has a microwave field.

249
00:16:16,458 --> 00:16:17,860
It's here that the

250
00:16:17,861 --> 00:16:20,542
atoms move into their excited state.

251
00:16:20,543 --> 00:16:25,160
The difference between those two states is given in terms of a

252
00:16:25,161 --> 00:16:33,000
frequency as 9,192,631,770 cycles per second, and this process is repeated

253
00:16:33,001 --> 00:16:37,000
24 hours a day, seven days a week for about a month.

254
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:40,440
The clock counts the cycles, or ticks.

255
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:49,900
And every 9,192,631,770 ticks is a second.

256
00:16:52,060 --> 00:16:55,140
The whole world is now dependent on this time measurement.

257
00:16:56,060 --> 00:17:00,740
You don't need time which is good to one second in 100 million years in your everyday life.

258
00:17:00,741 --> 00:17:05,360
except for systems that you tend not to know run on time.

259
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:10,000
Things like the global positioning system is absolutely dependent on atomic clocks.

260
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:14,060
High-speed data transfer over Internet lines and things like that

261
00:17:14,061 --> 00:17:16,940
depend on really accurate frequency representations

262
00:17:16,941 --> 00:17:20,360
because we have to agree about what the frequency of the transfer is.

263
00:17:21,460 --> 00:17:24,020
Of course, timekeeping wasn't always this accurate.

264
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:27,720
The earliest clocks were very rudimentary timers,

265
00:17:28,340 --> 00:17:30,300
and some examples can be found here.

266
00:17:30,740 --> 00:17:35,460
at the National Association of Watch and Clock Collectors Museum in Columbia, Pennsylvania.

267
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:41,980
Klepsidra, or water clocks, were used as early as the 2nd century BC in ancient Egypt.

268
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:45,780
As it empties, it either fills another basin to tell you how time it is,

269
00:17:45,880 --> 00:17:48,980
or by marks on the inside of the klepsidra, it tells you what time it is.

270
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:54,400
As the sun passes through the sky, sundials equated shadow to a measurement.

271
00:17:56,260 --> 00:17:59,540
Monasteries and churches burned candles and incense to keep time.

272
00:18:00,740 --> 00:18:04,000
and the mechanical clock wouldn't have advanced as early as it did

273
00:18:04,001 --> 00:18:09,040
if medieval religious institutions hadn't pushed for its creation in the 13th century.

274
00:18:10,640 --> 00:18:14,920
Religion has always played a really vital role in the measurement of time and keeping track of time.

275
00:18:15,040 --> 00:18:18,960
Each religion required you to know what time it was, so you knew it was time for you then to go to prayer.

276
00:18:18,961 --> 00:18:22,880
Some of the earliest time pieces were church clocks or tower clocks.

277
00:18:24,440 --> 00:18:29,460
In the 17th century, the pendulum revolutionized the way clocks measured time.

278
00:18:29,461 --> 00:18:34,240
While the pendulum was a huge advance in the accuracy of the clock, it did have problems.

279
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:36,260
One of the main problems is it's made out of metal,

280
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:40,120
but you have to compensate for changes in the metal as a result of temperature change.

281
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:43,360
One way to do that is using a mercury pendulum like the one you see here,

282
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:49,220
which when the rod expands from heat, the mercury will rise and maintain the swing,

283
00:18:49,300 --> 00:18:51,680
the balance of the pendulum, and the accuracy of the clock.

284
00:18:52,140 --> 00:18:55,140
Another way to do that is by putting two different metals together

285
00:18:55,141 --> 00:18:57,320
that react differently to temperature changes,

286
00:18:57,321 --> 00:18:59,240
therefore the pendulum stays the same length.

287
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:00,840
and it keeps the clock running accurately.

288
00:19:01,740 --> 00:19:04,780
We now return to Measure It on Modern Marbles.

289
00:19:08,060 --> 00:19:10,660
Hourglasses use the flow of sand to measure time.

290
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:14,480
Early sailors needed them to successfully navigate the seas.

291
00:19:15,140 --> 00:19:16,640
In discovery of the New World,

292
00:19:16,820 --> 00:19:19,040
sailing across the ocean was an important endeavor,

293
00:19:19,220 --> 00:19:21,060
and it was important not to get lost on the way.

294
00:19:21,061 --> 00:19:24,340
And it turns out that latitude, distance north and south,

295
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:26,880
was fairly well measured by tracking the sun,

296
00:19:26,881 --> 00:19:31,140
but longitude, the distance across the ocean, was a challenge.

297
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:35,120
To find longitude, sailors needed a portable timepiece

298
00:19:35,121 --> 00:19:37,000
to calculate their position at sea,

299
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:41,000
and pendulum clocks of the day couldn't operate without a stable surface.

300
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:46,660
In 1761, Yorkshire carpenter John Harrison,

301
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:51,560
with no scientific training, solved this problem with his marine chronometer.

302
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:56,700
It had a pair of counter-oscillating weighted beams connected by springs

303
00:19:56,701 --> 00:19:59,740
that weren't affected by gravity or the motion of a ship.

304
00:20:00,260 --> 00:20:04,140
It kept time accurate to five seconds a day, even in stormy seas.

305
00:20:04,980 --> 00:20:08,420
This technology remained ship standard well into the 20th century.

306
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:13,400
In the 16th century, clocks got smaller and more portable.

307
00:20:15,080 --> 00:20:19,540
Mechanical watches can be highly sophisticated, but they all measure time the same way.

308
00:20:19,541 --> 00:20:22,900
The first section is the mainspring.

309
00:20:22,901 --> 00:20:27,240
It's a coiled piece of steel that is pretty big when you get it all apart.

310
00:20:27,420 --> 00:20:28,440
That's our power source.

311
00:20:29,920 --> 00:20:32,280
Winding the watch puts tension on the mainspring.

312
00:20:33,840 --> 00:20:37,440
As the spring uncoils, it releases energy to the gear train.

313
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:43,820
The gear train transmits this energy to the balance wheel, which oscillates back and forth.

314
00:20:45,280 --> 00:20:48,140
The escapement keeps the balance wheel oscillating

315
00:20:48,141 --> 00:20:52,700
by allowing the watch's gears to advance a set amount with each swing of the balance.

316
00:20:52,900 --> 00:20:59,900
With each turn of the gears, the readable time is expressed with hands on the watch face.

317
00:20:59,901 --> 00:21:07,900
You'll pay upwards of $10,000 for a beautifully crafted mechanical watch that will measure accurate time within seconds every year.

318
00:21:07,901 --> 00:21:12,900
But what if you have $9,995 less?

319
00:21:12,901 --> 00:21:16,900
A battery-powered kids' quartz watch will do fine.

320
00:21:16,901 --> 00:21:20,900
It's actually 10 times more accurate than the finest mechanical watch.

321
00:21:20,901 --> 00:21:21,601
watch.

322
00:21:21,602 --> 00:21:26,100
It's the same portions and the same components that actually do the same thing

323
00:21:26,101 --> 00:21:27,169
as a mechanical watch.

324
00:21:27,170 --> 00:21:30,520
The quartz movement will keep better time just because the technology

325
00:21:30,521 --> 00:21:35,613
is so much more advanced. Here the mainspring is replaced by a battery.

326
00:21:35,614 --> 00:21:37,120
Instead of a balance,

327
00:21:37,121 --> 00:21:42,620
time is measured with the oscillations of an electrified piece of synthetic quartz.

328
00:21:42,621 --> 00:21:46,680
The pulse from the quartz is regulated by a circuit board, which changes the digital

329
00:21:46,681 --> 00:21:49,640
readout on the face.

330
00:21:49,740 --> 00:21:52,120
So there are lots of ways to measure time.

331
00:21:52,220 --> 00:21:53,920
Take your mark.

332
00:21:54,020 --> 00:21:54,920
Up!

333
00:21:55,020 --> 00:21:57,920
Ah!

334
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:01,320
But what if you need to stop it?

335
00:22:01,420 --> 00:22:04,040
Colorado Time Systems in Loveland, Colorado,

336
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:07,240
makes hyper-accurate timing systems and touch pads used

337
00:22:07,340 --> 00:22:10,440
for competitive swimming events.

338
00:22:10,540 --> 00:22:13,000
The system typically measures a swimmer's time within 1

339
00:22:13,100 --> 00:22:16,140
1-100th of a second.

340
00:22:16,141 --> 00:22:22,560
That's the interval by which Michael Phelps barely took gold in the 100-meter butterfly at the 2008 Beijing Olympics.

341
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:30,120
The touchpad only responds to the stroke of the swimmer's fingertips.

342
00:22:31,260 --> 00:22:33,100
Waves in the pool don't affect its measurements.

343
00:22:34,400 --> 00:22:38,100
Results this infallible have only been recorded since the mid-1980s.

344
00:22:38,980 --> 00:22:42,800
The early days, swimming was judged by humans.

345
00:22:42,801 --> 00:22:46,600
They were eyeballing the finish and declaring winners,

346
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:49,700
and you would have a number of judges each picking place.

347
00:22:49,880 --> 00:22:53,560
Then timing came along and manual stopwatches were used.

348
00:22:54,180 --> 00:22:55,760
But humans make mistakes.

349
00:22:56,620 --> 00:22:59,520
The timing system is now a fully automatic timing system.

350
00:22:59,640 --> 00:23:04,920
The start is picked up electronically when the referee hits the horn

351
00:23:04,921 --> 00:23:06,880
and the strobe light goes off.

352
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:08,580
As the swimmer touches the wall,

353
00:23:08,581 --> 00:23:12,180
we will record the exact time that that touch came in.

354
00:23:12,800 --> 00:23:14,920
The pad is essentially a large switch.

355
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:20,020
When a swimmer touches it, the switch closes and sends a signal to the timing system.

356
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:23,780
This collects the signals from the touchpads.

357
00:23:24,280 --> 00:23:27,400
It passes the data to our specialized timing board.

358
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:34,000
On the timing board is the single crystal that is the very accurate part of the timing.

359
00:23:34,720 --> 00:23:36,560
It's accurate to five parts per million.

360
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:41,120
And underneath the timing board is a standard single board computer.

361
00:23:41,121 --> 00:23:44,620
The timing system is wired directly into the scoreboard.

362
00:23:45,320 --> 00:23:51,960
Currently, all the rule books in the world specify that we should time to one hundredth of a second accuracy.

363
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:55,220
But we have the ability to time to a thousandth of a second.

364
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:59,000
This is used sometimes, particularly when people want to make sure ties are broken.

365
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:05,380
A timer can help calculate a swimmer's speed.

366
00:24:06,860 --> 00:24:08,900
But how do you measure speed in a plane?

367
00:24:08,901 --> 00:24:15,900
in a car or outside of a car.

368
00:24:15,901 --> 00:24:18,900
A jiffy is an actual unit of time.

369
00:24:18,901 --> 00:24:22,900
Computer scientists say it's about 1 1 100th of a second.

370
00:24:22,901 --> 00:24:26,900
Electrical engineers clock it at around 1 60th of a second.

371
00:24:26,901 --> 00:24:29,900
But physicists view it as the time light takes

372
00:24:29,901 --> 00:24:33,900
to travel the radius of an electron.

373
00:24:33,901 --> 00:24:38,900
Measure It will return on Modern Marvels.

374
00:24:38,901 --> 00:24:42,900
We now return to Measure It on Modern Marvels.

375
00:24:42,901 --> 00:24:45,900
If you can measure distance and you can measure time,

376
00:24:45,901 --> 00:24:47,900
you can measure speed.

377
00:24:47,901 --> 00:24:50,900
Most of us rely on only one speed measurement,

378
00:24:50,901 --> 00:24:53,900
the one displayed on our car's speedometer.

379
00:24:53,901 --> 00:24:56,900
We expect it to be accurate every time we drive.

380
00:24:56,901 --> 00:24:59,900
The speedometer is a display that indicates how fast

381
00:24:59,901 --> 00:25:01,900
your vehicle is moving.

382
00:25:01,901 --> 00:25:05,940
A speedometer picks up the speed that your tires are rotating,

383
00:25:06,480 --> 00:25:11,020
and it converts that into the speed your vehicle is moving down the road.

384
00:25:12,100 --> 00:25:14,960
General Motors Proving Grounds in Milford, Michigan,

385
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:19,800
utilizes over 130 miles of track to test its speedometers for errors.

386
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:25,200
What we're prepared to demonstrate here is how long it takes to cover

387
00:25:25,201 --> 00:25:28,620
the distance of a football field at a given speed.

388
00:25:28,621 --> 00:25:32,380
So in the simplest terms, distance divided by time,

389
00:25:32,500 --> 00:25:34,960
we're going to go through one time at 60 miles an hour

390
00:25:34,961 --> 00:25:36,580
and measure that speed.

391
00:25:36,940 --> 00:25:39,280
Then we'll go the next time at 150 miles an hour

392
00:25:39,281 --> 00:25:42,060
to show for the kind of differences you'll see

393
00:25:42,061 --> 00:25:44,580
in the amount of distance covered in time.

394
00:25:45,480 --> 00:25:48,740
We're approaching the pylons here at 60 miles an hour,

395
00:25:48,840 --> 00:25:49,820
so we'll get our time.

396
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:55,700
Start.

397
00:25:55,701 --> 00:26:04,232
stop. So that was roughly three seconds to go through 100 yards.

398
00:26:04,233 --> 00:26:07,240
At 150 miles per hour,

399
00:26:07,340 --> 00:26:09,800
a car will make the same trip in a little over one second.

400
00:26:10,240 --> 00:26:14,000
Now we're approaching a little bit quicker than last time.

401
00:26:16,380 --> 00:26:23,500
Here's the pylons, set, stop. That was one second.

402
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:31,120
Motorists can use this simple method to check the measurements

403
00:26:31,121 --> 00:26:32,220
on their car's speedometer.

404
00:26:32,221 --> 00:26:35,740
When you're going down the highway, there are mile markers,

405
00:26:35,741 --> 00:26:40,180
so you can set your crews on a given speed from one mile marker

406
00:26:40,181 --> 00:26:43,940
to the next, take a stopwatch, and say, for example,

407
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:49,020
it takes 45 seconds to go from one mile marker to the next.

408
00:26:49,560 --> 00:26:51,720
That means you're going 80 miles an hour.

409
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:55,160
Fortunately, a speedometer does the calculating for us.

410
00:26:55,480 --> 00:27:01,180
Its real-time reporting of a vehicle's speed begins with wheel speed sensors in the bearing of each tire.

411
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:07,640
That data from the wheel speed transfers up to an engine control module like this.

412
00:27:07,680 --> 00:27:09,240
This is basically a mini-computer.

413
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:14,440
This actually calculates the RPK, revolutions per kilometer, for this tire,

414
00:27:15,020 --> 00:27:18,420
and it calculates the absolute speed of the vehicle going down the road.

415
00:27:18,860 --> 00:27:24,620
So that speed is then transmitted from this box to the cluster that you actually look at.

416
00:27:25,480 --> 00:27:29,480
Speedometers like this one only became available in the 1980s.

417
00:27:29,481 --> 00:27:32,480
Before we were as advanced electronically as we are,

418
00:27:32,481 --> 00:27:37,480
this would have been a cable coming into the back of the speedometer and actually rotating.

419
00:27:37,481 --> 00:27:40,480
And instead of using the computer to calculate the speed,

420
00:27:40,481 --> 00:27:45,480
there were sets of gears that mechanically did the conversion for you.

421
00:27:45,481 --> 00:27:52,480
Despite technological differences, the precise calibration of both systems depends on the diameter of the tires.

422
00:27:52,481 --> 00:28:00,380
A bigger tire will rotate fewer times over the same distance that a smaller tire will.

423
00:28:00,980 --> 00:28:07,080
So to get the calculations correct so that your speed indicates correctly, you just take that into account.

424
00:28:07,081 --> 00:28:12,200
The lesson? Change your tire size and your speedometer will no longer be accurate.

425
00:28:13,080 --> 00:28:20,660
In the GM Tire and Wheel Lab, engineers use a dynamometer to try to replicate tire stress as equivalent to real road conditions.

426
00:28:20,661 --> 00:28:25,660
This machine simulates the load that this axle would see for a given car.

427
00:28:25,661 --> 00:28:34,660
This point changes with how heavy the vehicle is, how soft the tire is, how much heat is in the tire.

428
00:28:34,661 --> 00:28:41,660
So this is a way for us to accurately calculate what this radius is, which we call the static loaded radius,

429
00:28:41,661 --> 00:28:46,660
and that ultimately tells us what the revolutions per kilometer are.

430
00:28:46,661 --> 00:28:52,660
This RPK measurement is used to calculate speed in the engine control module.

431
00:28:52,661 --> 00:29:00,660
Speedometers obviously have a certain amount of error, but we try to keep our error within plus or minus a mile per hour

432
00:29:00,661 --> 00:29:06,660
when you're out at 60 or 70 miles per hour so that you know exactly what your speed is.

433
00:29:06,661 --> 00:29:10,660
In new cars, speedometers are more important than ever.

434
00:29:10,661 --> 00:29:14,160
When vehicles had a lot of wind noise or a lot of engine noise,

435
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:18,860
a lot of road noise, it was obvious very quickly how fast you were going.

436
00:29:19,560 --> 00:29:22,760
And now when you're in the most refined vehicles,

437
00:29:23,180 --> 00:29:25,640
you look down and you're surprised how fast you're actually going,

438
00:29:25,820 --> 00:29:27,520
which is why the speedometer is very important.

439
00:29:28,280 --> 00:29:32,180
All fine for us car drivers, but how does a pilot know how fast he's going?

440
00:29:33,240 --> 00:29:36,480
As it flies, a jet measures ram pressure,

441
00:29:36,740 --> 00:29:39,520
or the pressure of air molecules hitting the jet's body.

442
00:29:39,521 --> 00:29:42,520
The jet also measures air pressure.

443
00:29:42,521 --> 00:29:48,520
The difference between ram pressure and the air pressure is indicated to the pilot as the jet's speed.

444
00:29:48,521 --> 00:29:52,520
Pilots refer to their mock meters during supersonic flight.

445
00:29:52,521 --> 00:29:57,520
The mock speed is the jet's speed divided by the speed of sound.

446
00:29:57,521 --> 00:30:03,520
Back on Earth, your speedometer is not the only thing measuring how fast your car is going on the highway.

447
00:30:03,521 --> 00:30:07,520
Officers use radar to track speeders on our roads.

448
00:30:07,521 --> 00:30:11,520
Sergeant Andrew Hernandez with the California Highway Patrol

449
00:30:11,521 --> 00:30:14,520
trains officers how to use the technology.

450
00:30:14,521 --> 00:30:18,520
Back in 1842, Christian Johann Doppler theorized

451
00:30:18,521 --> 00:30:21,520
the Doppler principle, and the Doppler radar is what is used

452
00:30:21,521 --> 00:30:23,520
in police radar.

453
00:30:23,521 --> 00:30:26,520
Radar uses radio waves to measure speed.

454
00:30:26,521 --> 00:30:29,520
The radio wave is infinite unless one of three things

455
00:30:29,521 --> 00:30:32,520
happens, and that's either refraction, refraction, or

456
00:30:32,521 --> 00:30:36,520
absorption. So when that happens, when it attacks a vehicle

457
00:30:36,521 --> 00:30:40,360
vehicle that is coming at it, it's going to go ahead and have what they call a Doppler ship.

458
00:30:40,361 --> 00:30:43,625
If the vehicle is coming at it, it normally compresses the waves.

459
00:30:43,626 --> 00:30:44,680
Pulling away from it,

460
00:30:44,681 --> 00:30:46,110
it'll elongate the wave.

461
00:30:46,111 --> 00:30:50,280
The patrol car's radar unit sends out radio waves to confirm the speed

462
00:30:50,281 --> 00:30:52,766
of the patrol car relative to the ground.

463
00:30:52,767 --> 00:30:56,040
The waves also reflect off other vehicles on the road,

464
00:30:56,600 --> 00:31:00,520
and the radar unit records the difference in frequency, or Doppler shift.

465
00:31:00,521 --> 00:31:05,820
That's all the unit needs to calculate the speed of the target vehicle.

466
00:31:07,020 --> 00:31:11,280
And the frequency shift recognized by the radar unit is audible to the officer.

467
00:31:11,800 --> 00:31:17,320
On the right would be the patrol car speed, and on the left would be the target vehicle speed.

468
00:31:17,460 --> 00:31:20,020
For example, if we're picking up the big rig, we have 48 miles an hour.

469
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:24,840
It doesn't come up and say this speed belongs to this truck.

470
00:31:24,841 --> 00:31:30,980
That's where the expertise and the years in the job,

471
00:31:31,220 --> 00:31:33,980
being able to do a visual estimation of people's speeds comes into play.

472
00:31:34,160 --> 00:31:37,680
The radar unit is just a tool for the officers to verify what they're seeing.

473
00:31:38,380 --> 00:31:39,840
But when the unit detects a speeder...

474
00:31:45,920 --> 00:31:49,020
The reason why I'm stopping is the post is speeding, but it's 65,

475
00:31:49,260 --> 00:31:51,140
and I'm having a radar at 80 miles an hour.

