00:42:32,821 --> 00:42:35,820
Fahrenheit took his measurements with mercury in glass,

647
00:42:35,821 --> 00:42:38,820
which was very conducive to temperature change.

648
00:42:38,821 --> 00:42:42,820
Mercury responds to a change in temperature more quickly

649
00:42:42,821 --> 00:42:44,820
than other liquids.

650
00:42:44,821 --> 00:42:48,820
The thermometer freezes at minus 36 degrees Fahrenheit,

651
00:42:48,821 --> 00:42:50,820
but it does go over a thousand degrees.

652
00:42:50,821 --> 00:42:54,820
It is a very usable but a very dangerous liquid.

653
00:42:54,821 --> 00:42:58,820
Today, many thermometers are made with alcohol or kerosene.

654
00:42:58,821 --> 00:43:03,820
They work the same, minus the health hazards.

655
00:43:03,821 --> 00:43:09,820
NIST establishes its calibration standard with something called a water triple point cell.

656
00:43:09,821 --> 00:43:12,820
This is a cell that has only very pure water in it.

657
00:43:12,821 --> 00:43:14,820
Up here there's no air, it's a vacuum.

658
00:43:14,821 --> 00:43:19,820
When water exists in all three phases of matter, it's at the triple point.

659
00:43:19,821 --> 00:43:27,820
Whenever we have those three phases coexisting, then that nature conspires to lock that down at a single temperature.

660
00:43:27,821 --> 00:43:31,440
We've assigned that temperature to be 0.01 degrees C.

661
00:43:31,540 --> 00:43:33,540
This is the point that defines the Kelvin.

662
00:43:34,540 --> 00:43:36,040
The Kelvin is the NIST standard,

